</style></head><body><header class="top"><div class="wrap"><div class="logo"><div class="mark">851</div><div>MSK CLINICAL REASONING LAB<div class="eyebrow">STUDENT PILOT · V0.9</div></div></div><div class="pill">SIX-CASE PILOT</div></div></header><div class="wrap intro"><div class="eyebrow">CLINICAL DECISION MAKING · 2026 PILOT</div><h1>Where would you draw the line?</h1><p>Consider the evidence, the potential harm of delay, and what can safely be done now. More restrictive is not automatically more correct.</p></div><div class="wrap layout"><aside class="panel aside"><div class="mini">Your case sequence</div><div class="caseNav" id="pilotNav"></div><div id="syncStatus" role="status" aria-live="polite" class="syncSummary"></div><small>Six cases · Two checkpoints each<br>No grade · No answers revealed during cases</small></aside><main class="panel main" id="screen" aria-live="polite"></main></div><div class="wrap foot">MSK 851 · Fictional educational cases · Course disposition framework, not a validated diagnostic instrument. Faculty review required before wider use.</div><script>
(function(){'use strict';
const ALL=[{"id":"C01","title":"First visit","region":"Lumbar","stage1":"A 53-year-old warehouse employee presents with low back pain that is worse after long shifts and eases with walking. They ask whether they can begin a light exercise plan today. During intake, their blood pressure is 158/96 mm Hg. They take medication for hypertension but missed a recent follow-up visit.","stage2":"After sitting quietly, blood pressure is 152/92 mm Hg. The patient feels as they normally do and has no new symptoms to report. The planned visit involves education, comfortable mobility and self-paced walking."},{"id":"C02","title":"The shoulder consultation","region":"Shoulder / thoracic","stage1":"A 61-year-old patient reports an ache near the left shoulder blade. Shoulder movement is a little stiff, but the ache is also noticed when carrying groceries upstairs and tends to settle after resting. They ask whether strengthening would help.","stage2":"While walking a short distance in the clinic, the discomfort returns with a new pressure across the chest. The patient stops and says they feel clammy."},{"id":"C03","title":"Mornings are difficult","region":"Lumbopelvic","stage1":"A 35-year-old patient seeks help for low back and buttock discomfort that has fluctuated for a year. Mornings are difficult, whereas a walk before work often helps. Long periods at a desk make symptoms more noticeable. The referral simply says chronic low back pain.","stage2":"During a discussion of other health concerns, the patient mentions intermittent psoriasis plaques and asks whether they could be connected to the back symptoms. They remain able to participate in self-paced exercise."},{"id":"C04","title":"After the weekend","region":"Thoracolumbar","stage1":"A 72-year-old patient has pain near the thoracolumbar junction that began when lifting a full laundry basket four days ago. Rolling in bed and standing from a chair are especially uncomfortable. There was no memorable fall. The patient hopes some hands-on treatment will help them return to gardening.","stage2":"During medication review, you discover several years of oral glucocorticoid use. Asked to locate the worst pain, the patient points with one finger to the midline near the thoracolumbar junction."},{"id":"C05","title":"After moving furniture","region":"Lumbar","stage1":"A 45-year-old patient reports ten days of low back pain with intermittent tingling in both thighs following a weekend of moving furniture. They walk independently, and the symptoms vary with position.","stage2":"As the visit is ending, the patient mentions that since yesterday they have needed to strain to begin urinating. They also noticed that sensation while wiping after toileting feels different."},{"id":"C06","title":"The follow-up appointment","region":"Knee / lower leg","stage1":"A 57-year-old patient arrives three weeks after knee replacement. Their knee motion is gradually improving, but the operated-side calf has felt tight since yesterday and their sock leaves a deeper mark near the ankle. They would like to continue the usual rehabilitation session.","stage2":"Comparing the legs, you notice substantially more calf swelling on the operated side and tenderness not explained by knee movement. The patient has not been assessed for a possible clot."}], SET='Unified', LS='msk851_pilot_v09_six_cases';
const cases=ALL;
const COLLECTOR_URL='https://script.google.com/macros/s/AKfycbyv7lJLgWUnWhLzUqOKcCCpAQ8mZ-Jf0LdUYfUq5bTJO6A2TdfryJxNA9mZAW1mvWE/exec'; // Faculty setup generates the deployable student file.
const IS_CONNECTED=/^https:\/\/script\.google\.com\/macros\/s\/[^\s]+\/exec$/.test(COLLECTOR_URL);
const REVIEWS={"C01":{"initial":"Yellow is a reasonable working plan; an Orange hold could be justified if symptoms, planned exertion, or local BP precautions have not yet been clarified.","final":"y","why":"On repeat measurement the BP remains elevated but is lower, the patient feels at baseline, and the proposed session is gentle. Monitor and adapt PT, document findings, and coordinate appropriate hypertension follow-up. The reading alone is not proof of emergency.","trap":"Neither ignore the BP nor automatically treat a moderately elevated value as an emergency.","pivots":[{"id":"C01_P1","color":"g","change":"The repeat BP is in the patient’s usual controlled range (for example, 126/78 mm Hg); there is no new concern requiring a separate medical referral.","why":"Routine, symptom-guided PT can proceed. A missed routine follow-up still merits a reminder, but does not necessarily create a special referral requirement."},{"id":"C01_P2","color":"o","change":"The properly repeated resting BP is markedly elevated (for example, 194/116 mm Hg), without acute symptoms.","why":"Defer exercise pending prompt medical clarification and follow local BP policy; do not call an asymptomatic number alone an emergency."},{"id":"C01_P3","color":"r","change":"During the visit, the patient develops sudden unilateral weakness or new crushing chest discomfort.","why":"An acute possible stroke or cardiac event changes the pathway to an emergency response, irrespective of the original back-pain complaint."}]},"C02":{"initial":"Orange—stop exertional PT and arrange prompt medical assessment—is the principal initial action for an exertional symptom relieved by rest; do not treat it as purely a shoulder complaint.","final":"r","why":"New active chest pressure with diaphoresis during exertion is concerning for an acute coronary syndrome. Stop activity and activate the local emergency response immediately.","trap":"Pain near the scapula or slight shoulder stiffness must not override the exertional nature of the symptom.","pivots":[{"id":"C02_P1","color":"g","change":"A completed, appropriate medical evaluation has excluded ischemic disease as the cause, and pain is now limited to reproducible shoulder loading.","why":"When the cardiac concern is actually excluded, routine orthopedic treatment can be reasonable with normal reassessment."},{"id":"C02_P2","color":"y","change":"Medical evaluation has excluded an acute coronary process, but cardiovascular risk-factor follow-up is still needed; shoulder loading is otherwise tolerated.","why":"Manage the appropriate musculoskeletal component while communicating about routine risk-factor follow-up, rather than prescribing a blanket halt to all PT."},{"id":"C02_P3","color":"o","change":"The new chest pressure and clamminess never occur; only recurrent exertional shoulder-blade discomfort relieved by rest remains.","why":"The possible ischemic pattern still calls for stopping exertional PT and prompt medical evaluation, but does not automatically establish an active emergency at this moment."}]},"C03":{"initial":"Yellow is usually reasonable with an active differential and medical follow-up; the symptom pattern alone does not make all exercise unsafe.","final":"y","why":"The chronic inflammatory-sounding pattern and psoriasis increase the likelihood of an inflammatory rheumatologic condition. Coordinate appropriate medical assessment while continuing well-tolerated exercise when safe.","trap":"Do not make a diagnosis from one pattern or assume any inflammatory possibility means PT must stop.","pivots":[{"id":"C03_P1","color":"g","change":"A prior appropriate rheumatologic evaluation has excluded inflammatory disease, and there are no other concerns beyond manageable mechanical symptoms.","why":"Routine exercise-based care can continue without an additional referral from this presentation."},{"id":"C03_P2","color":"o","change":"Instead, the patient has a new unexplained systemic illness with persistent fever and feels acutely unwell at the visit.","why":"Defer the planned session and organize prompt medical assessment of possible infection or other systemic disease. The safety of exercise is not established."},{"id":"C03_P3","color":"r","change":"A new episode of urinary retention occurs together with altered perineal sensation.","why":"Those new neurologic features supersede the chronic pain pattern and call for emergency cauda equina evaluation."}]},"C04":{"initial":"Orange is defensible given older age and new focal pain after low loading; a cautious Yellow plan may be arguable only with an explicit screening and loading restriction plan.","final":"o","why":"Long-term glucocorticoid exposure plus focal midline pain after minimal loading raises concern for vertebral fragility fracture. Defer potentially provocative spinal loading or manipulation and arrange prompt medical evaluation.","trap":"An absent fall or a patient able to walk does not exclude a compression fracture.","pivots":[{"id":"C04_P1","color":"g","change":"An appropriate medical assessment has already ruled out an acute fracture and confirmed an uncomplicated, improving muscular injury.","why":"With that concern addressed, graded PT may proceed without a new referral for this complaint; continue ordinary reassessment."},{"id":"C04_P2","color":"y","change":"A clinician has ruled out an acute vertebral fracture, but the patient’s long-term glucocorticoid-related bone health has not been reviewed.","why":"Treat appropriately for the evaluated musculoskeletal problem while coordinating routine bone-health assessment rather than automatically suspending all PT."},{"id":"C04_P3","color":"r","change":"The patient now develops rapidly progressive leg weakness with new urinary retention.","why":"New severe neurologic changes create a potential compressive spinal emergency, requiring emergency evaluation rather than routine imaging referral."}]},"C05":{"initial":"Yellow or Orange may be defensible initially, depending on the safety screen and immediate plan; bilateral symptoms should trigger targeted bladder, perineal, and motor questions.","final":"r","why":"New urinary initiation difficulty together with altered perineal sensation raises concern for possible cauda equina syndrome. Initiate the local emergency assessment pathway; do not wait for complete incontinence or paralysis.","trap":"Do not rely on an unremarkable gait or positional symptom variability to negate newly reported bladder/perineal change.","pivots":[{"id":"C05_P1","color":"g","change":"The concerning bladder and perineal history is evaluated promptly and found unrelated; the clinical work-up identifies uncomplicated, improving mechanical symptoms without residual neurologic concern.","why":"Once an appropriate evaluation has actually resolved the emergency concern, usual conservative PT can resume. Simply denying a symptom without evaluating it is not equivalent."},{"id":"C05_P2","color":"y","change":"Instead of a new bladder or perineal change, the patient describes only brief position-dependent thigh tingling, with a reassuring focused neurologic screen.","why":"A proportionate PT plan with follow-up and appropriate medical coordination if the symptoms persist can be reasonable. A neurologic symptom does not always require PT to stop."},{"id":"C05_P3","color":"o","change":"Instead of urinary or perineal change, testing reveals new objective ankle dorsiflexion weakness that is stable rather than rapidly worsening.","why":"The unresolved motor deficit warrants prompt medical clarification and careful modification or deferral of planned treatment, even without a cauda equina presentation."}]},"C06":{"initial":"Orange is the safer provisional plan when new unilateral calf symptoms appear after knee replacement until the possibility of DVT is meaningfully assessed.","final":"o","why":"Recent major surgery plus new unilateral calf swelling and tenderness raises suspicion for DVT. Withhold routine limb treatment and initiate the local prompt diagnostic/referral pathway; the patient does not need PE symptoms for DVT to be considered.","trap":"Do not apply vigorous massage or routine loading while an unresolved postoperative clot concern is being assessed.","pivots":[{"id":"C06_P1","color":"g","change":"A timely, appropriate DVT evaluation is negative, and the current symptoms are documented as expected postoperative change.","why":"The suspected clot has been assessed; routine postoperative rehabilitation can proceed according to clinical tolerance and surgical precautions."},{"id":"C06_P2","color":"y","change":"DVT evaluation is negative, but the swelling remains increased and the surgeon requests nonurgent follow-up.","why":"Modify activity as necessary and coordinate ongoing postoperative care while treatment continues appropriately."},{"id":"C06_P3","color":"r","change":"The patient suddenly develops unexplained breathlessness with chest pain or collapses.","why":"A possible pulmonary embolism or other acute cardiopulmonary event warrants immediate emergency response."}]}};
